    "text": "<<The History of the World, as Told by Dogs>>  \n\n**Proposal for a New University Course: \"The History of the World, as Told by Dogs\"**  \n\n**Course Title:** <<The History of the World, as Told by Dogs>>  \n\n**Course Code:** HIST-499  \n\n**Instructor:** Dr. Emily Carter, Professor of History and Ethics  \n\n**Semester:** Fall 2025  \n\n**Duration:** 15 weeks, 3 hours per week  \n\n**Credit Hours:** 3  \n\n---\n\n**Course Overview:**  \n\n*The History of the World, as Told by Dogs* is a unique and engaging interdisciplinary course that explores the history of the world through the lens of dogs—both as historical subjects and as cultural symbols. This course will examine how dogs have shaped human civilization, from ancient times to the present, and how they have, in turn, been shaped by human societies. Through a combination of historical analysis, literary interpretation, and ethical reflection, students will explore the complex relationship between humans and their canine companions.  \n\n---\n\n**Course Objectives:**  \n\n1. To understand the historical role of dogs in human societies, including their use in hunting, warfare, and companionship.  \n2. To analyze how dogs have influenced cultural narratives, literature, and art throughout history.  \n3. To explore the ethical and philosophical implications of human-dog relationships.  \n4. To develop critical thinking and analytical skills through the study of historical texts and primary sources.  \n\n---\n\n**Course Structure:**  \n\n- **Week 1:** Introduction to the course, the role of dogs in human history, and the concept of \"history as told by dogs.\"  \n- **Weeks 2–4:** Ancient civilizations and the domestication of dogs.  \n- **Weeks 5–8:** The Middle Ages and the role of dogs in warfare and religion.  \n- **Weeks 9–12:** The Renaissance and the Enlightenment, with a focus on dog imagery in art and literature.  \n- **Weeks 13–15:** Modern and contemporary history, including the rise of the dog as a cultural icon and the ethical debates surrounding pet ownership.  \n\n---\n\n**Course Content:**  \n\n- **Ancient Civilizations:** The domestication of dogs in Mesopotamia, Egypt, and the Indus Valley.  \n- **Warfare and Military Use:** Dogs in ancient warfare, including the use of hounds in the Roman army and the role of dogs",
